  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a route for making a freestanding single-crystalline membrane using a 2D material and remote epitaxy. It starts with a conventional single-crystal substrate coated by an atomically thin 2D layer, drawn as a dark hexagonal lattice. An epitaxial film is then grown on top of this 2D layer, indicating that the crystal below still guides the growth even though the film is not directly bonded to the substrate in the usual way.\n\nThe key step is a transfer-like release process labeled 2DLT. A curved stack consisting of thermal release tape and a metal stressor is brought onto the grown film. As this stressed layer peels upward, it mechanically lifts the epitaxial film away from the substrate while the 2D interlayer remains visible at the interface. The final product is shown as a bent, detached thin membrane, emphasizing that the grown crystal can be separated intact and handled as a flexible freestanding sheet.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a manufacturing sequence: single-crystal template → remote epitaxial growth through a 2D interlayer → stress-assisted delamination → reusable substrate and released crystalline membrane.",
